From 9ffb4fba7b3dc92972cf49da7a2d8dac18649444 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 01:21:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix spaces and tabs between '#' and a preprocessor directive
 name

C allows spaces and tabs between the '#' and the directive name
("# define", "#  ifdef", "# endif"), in active code and inside skipped
#if groups. The scanner read the directive name straight after the '#',
so such a line became a stray '#' token in active code and an
unrecognised directive in a skipped group, which was then not closed
where it should be.

Skip spaces and tabs before reading the name, in both NextRawToken and
NextSkipRawToken. The skip stays within the current text and leaves the
blank that ends a macro expansion in place, so a macro body ending in
'#' does not pick up a directive name from the text that follows the
expansion. It is not done in assembler macro text, where "# x" is an
immediate operand. "# #" remains two tokens. The same path handles the
stringify operator, so "# x" in a macro body now behaves like "#x".

The test covers indented directives in active and skipped groups,
nested #if/#ifdef/#elif/#else/#endif with depth tracking, #include,
#undef, "# x" against "#x", "##", and '#' immediates in assembler
macro text.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01J5rsu7nnbQYnRY7GP34gdn
